  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-21",
    "title": "Voyager's Neptune",
    "explanation": " Explanation:  Cruising through the outer solar system, the Voyager 2 spacecraft made its closest  approach to Neptune  on August 25, 1989, the only spacecraft to visit the most distant  ice giant planet . Based on the images recorded during its close encounter and in the following days,  this inspired composited scene  covers the dim outer planet, largest  moon Triton , and faint system of rings. From just beyond Neptune's orbit, the interplanetary perspective looks back toward the Sun, capturing the planet and Triton as thin  sunlit crescents .  Cirrus clouds  and a dark band circle Neptune's south polar region, with a cloudy vortex above the pole  itself . Parts of the  very faint ring system  along with the three bright ring arcs were first imaged by Voyager during the fly-by, though the faintest segments are modeled in this composited picture. Spanning 7.5 degrees, the background starfield is composed from sky survey data centered on the constellation Camelopardalis, corresponding to the outbound Voyager's view of the  magnificent Neptunian system . Still cruising in 2026, the Voyager 2 spacecraft is  now in interstellar space .   Tomorrow's picture:  Sunday's Childe",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Neptune-South-Pole-Voyager-2_2327x1670.jpg?w=2327&h=1670&fit=clip",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-21-voyagers-neptune/",
    "credit": "Assembly/Processing -  Rolf Olsen , Data -  Voyager 2 ,  NASA Planetary Data System ",
    "alt": "Composite Voyager image of Neptune in a crescent phase.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-11-21T09:10:22.201424+00:00"
